# 4. TREE GROWTH SYSTEM

This is the most important functional and visual feature.

Every tree must grow according to the age of its corresponding publication.

Growth must be deterministic and based on the publication timestamp.

For example:

- A post published today produces a seedling.
- A post published one week ago produces a small young plant.
- A post published one month ago produces a recognizable young tree.
- A post published six months ago produces a developing tree.
- A post published one year ago produces a substantially developed tree.
- A post published several years ago produces a mature tree.

These are illustrative stages. Design a configurable growth curve that makes growth visually satisfying.

## Technical implementation

Calculate tree age using:

current_time - post.published_at

Convert the resulting duration into a normalized growth parameter between 0 and 1.

Use this parameter to control:

- Trunk height.
- Trunk thickness.
- Branch development.
- Canopy size.
- Number and density of leaves.
- Overall tree silhouette.

Growth should be smooth and continuous rather than jumping between disconnected models.

Use optimized geometry or prebuilt growth-stage representations where appropriate.

The visual result should be consistent every time the same post is loaded.

Do not make trees randomly change size every time a visitor opens the website.

## Growth animation

When the forest initially loads, trees can gradually reveal their growth.

Newly planted trees should have a particularly beautiful planting animation:

1. A small seed appears.
2. The seed enters the ground.
3. A small shoot emerges.
4. The trunk develops.
5. Branches and leaves unfold.

Keep this animation short and visually elegant.

It should not replay every time a visitor clicks around the forest.

Provide a setting to enable or disable introductory animations.

## Tree appearance and species selection

Allow the administrator to select a tree species when publishing an article.

Provide an automatic selection option based on the article category.

The administrator must always be able to override the automatic selection.

Each tree must retain its species even after the website is restarted.

# 5. FOREST ORGANIZATION

The forest should not be a random collection of trees.

Develop a spatial organization system.

Possible organizational principles:

- Categories occupy distinct regions.
- Related posts grow relatively close to one another.
- Trees are distributed naturally.
- Older trees can occupy established forest areas.
- New trees can appear in recently developed areas.

However, avoid making the forest look like a rigid grid.

Use deterministic procedural placement.

Each post should have persistent X/Z coordinates stored in the database.

Adding a new article should not rearrange the entire forest.

When the administrator publishes a new article:

1. Create the database record.
2. Assign the article a tree species.
3. Calculate its initial growth.
4. Generate a suitable position.
5. Save the position.
6. Introduce the new tree into the forest.
7. Display a planting animation.

The tree must remain in the same position across subsequent visits.

